        "bodyTr": "President Lee Jae-myung has drawn controversy over expressions he used while discussing foreign workers and multicultural families. Some of his remarks deviated from factual accuracy, and certain statements have been criticized for appearing to divide Koreans and foreigners, which was deemed inappropriate. During a Cabinet meeting on the 27th, President Lee said, \"Bringing in foreign workers does not significantly benefit the national economy, because they send all the money back to their home countries.\" When progressive-leaning media outlets published critical coverage, President Lee demanded an apology, claiming the quote was inaccurate. This has sparked ongoing controversy, including resistance from the media company's labor union. The Korean Confederation of Trade Unions issued a statement condemning the president's hateful remarks, saying, \"We strongly denounce the president's discriminatory comments that demean migrant workers as 'remittance machines' and justify discrimination.\"\n\nForeign workers contribute significantly to the national economy. According to the Institute for Immigration Policy, foreign workers generated an economic ripple effect of 162 trillion won for the Korean economy this year. More important than the figure is that foreign workers are essential personnel in agriculture, fisheries, livestock farming, and small and medium-sized manufacturing facilities, given that Korean workers avoid 3D jobs. Moreover, according to statistics from the National Data Agency and the Ministry of Justice, as of May last year, the remittance ratio relative to foreign workers' total income was 25.5 percent. Therefore, the president's claim that they send all their money back is factually inaccurate. Even if most funds were remitted, it would be inappropriate for the president to raise concerns about it. According to UN conventions, migrant workers have the right to send remittances, and host countries are obligated to facilitate transfers. President Lee's indication on the 28th that he would consider abolishing certain local governments' preferential booking policies for maternity care facilities for multicultural families, in response to an internet post criticizing such policies as excessive, has also drawn backlash from progressive circles for dividing Koreans and foreigners. With a foreign resident population of three million on the horizon and the number of migrant workers exceeding 1.1 million, the president inviting criticism over discrimination against foreign workers and multicultural families serves no national interest whatsoever.",
