        "bodyTr": "Defense Minister Kang Shin-chul held his first Korea-U.S. defense ministerial talks the day before yesterday, but the two countries continued their standoff over the transfer of wartime operational control. Ultimately, the Korea-U.S. Security Consultative Meeting, or SCM, which had been held annually between the two countries, was postponed to next year for the first time in 28 years. Instead, the two sides agreed to hold defense ministerial talks in Washington in December, but the fact that the symbolic annual SCM of the Korea-U.S. alliance will be skipped reveals the current state of an alliance showing signs of discord. A year has passed since the Korean and U.S. leaders agreed last year on follow-up discussions regarding South Korea's nuclear submarine construction and permission for uranium enrichment and reprocessing, yet no progress has been made. The South Korean government had intended to lock in a specific timeline for the transfer—ideally within President Donald Trump's tenure, which ends in 2028, or at the latest within President Lee Jae-myung's tenure, which ends in 2030. However, the joint statement issued after the talks states that \"the two countries agreed to cooperate so that the transfer of wartime operational control can proceed smoothly at an 'appropriate' time.\" A U.S. Defense Department official said, \"The two countries continue to uphold the (existing) commitment to ensure that necessary conditions are met before the transfer takes place.\" The United States is maintaining its position that meeting conditions, not timing, is what matters. Prospects for the December defense ministerial meeting in Washington are also dim. The transfer of wartime operational control is an issue that redefines the status of the future Korea-U.S. alliance. The transfer is a point of collision between South Korea's desire to avoid the risk of \"entrapment\" in potential U.S.-China military conflicts and the United States' concern that U.S. forces in Korea will face constraints on strategic flexibility after the transfer. As such, compromise and concession are difficult. The transfer of wartime operational control can only happen, as Minister Kang stated, with trust between Korea and the United States, and it can succeed. It is not something that will happen simply because one side wants it. We must soberly recognize that the security threat landscape has changed due to strengthened North Korea-China-Russia coordination and North Korea's advancing nuclear and missile capabilities, and we must strengthen our preparations for the transfer. A pushy logic that would resort to independent action if the United States does not agree to an early transfer cannot successfully guide cold and meticulous security negotiations. The first step toward successfully achieving the transfer of wartime operational control is to restore mutual trust with the United States.",
